WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.200
Welcome back to Building
Ticket-to-Machine.

2
00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:06.338
This is Chapter 2: the ServiceNow
client and least privilege.

3
00:00:06.338 --> 00:00:09.371
An integration user with
more roles than its calls

4
00:00:09.371 --> 00:00:12.188
need is a standing
privilege nobody requested.

5
00:00:12.188 --> 00:00:16.801
So this chapter writes down every ServiceNow
call the kit's Python clients make,

6
00:00:16.801 --> 00:00:19.947
the task that makes it and
the least privilege it needs,

7
00:00:19.947 --> 00:00:22.813
and then makes the tests
hold the code to that list.

8
00:00:22.813 --> 00:00:26.104
We'll see how the clients
authenticate and page,

9
00:00:26.104 --> 00:00:31.020
the call list and its test, the three
roles, two labs and the dangers.

10
00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:36.145
Why write the calls down?

11
00:00:36.145 --> 00:00:40.045
Because privilege that nobody can
trace back to a call tends to grow.

12
00:00:40.045 --> 00:00:44.808
The kit's read me listed the roles in prose,
and nothing tied that list to the code.

13
00:00:44.808 --> 00:00:49.074
So a new call in a client would
surface as an error on a live instance,

14
00:00:49.074 --> 00:00:53.496
and the quickest fix, granting a
broader role, is exactly the wrong one.

15
00:00:53.496 --> 00:00:58.835
After this chapter, every call lives in
one file with its task and least privilege,

16
00:00:58.835 --> 00:01:03.820
a test holds the code to it, and a tool
compares a real grant with the list.

17
00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:09.645
Here's what the clients already share.

18
00:01:09.645 --> 00:01:12.163
Configuration comes from the environment,

19
00:01:12.163 --> 00:01:15.170
and a settings file that
carries a secret is refused.

20
00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:19.113
The token comes from a vault if one
is set, otherwise from the OAuth

21
00:01:19.113 --> 00:01:20.719
client-credentials grant,

22
00:01:20.719 --> 00:01:24.370
and only on a developer instance
from basic authentication.

23
00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:28.357
Paging reads a hundred rows
a page through the Table API.

24
00:01:28.357 --> 00:01:34.407
And one wrapper retries throttling and
unavailable responses, honoring Retry-After.

25
00:01:34.407 --> 00:01:38.595
The clients make three kinds of
ServiceNow call: posting findings,

26
00:01:38.595 --> 00:01:42.240
closing them with evidence,
and reading closed ones.

27
00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:49.365
This is the file the chapter
adds: one row per call.

28
00:01:49.365 --> 00:01:54.652
Every task first gets a token from its
own OAuth client and integration user.

29
00:01:54.652 --> 00:01:57.116
The drift push posts findings through the

30
00:01:57.116 --> 00:01:59.791
scripted REST resource,
as the finding writer.

31
00:01:59.791 --> 00:02:02.500
The re-read task lists waiting findings,

32
00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:07.428
patches each one and uploads an evidence
attachment, all as the verifier.

33
00:02:07.428 --> 00:02:11.328
And the evidence export reads the
same table under its own role,

34
00:02:11.328 --> 00:02:15.003
the evidence reader, which can
read but not change anything.

35
00:02:15.003 --> 00:02:18.520
Every row names the vendor
page that justifies it.

36
00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:25.345
The roles are custom roles
in the scoped application,

37
00:02:25.345 --> 00:02:27.582
and splitting them by task is the point.

38
00:02:27.582 --> 00:02:30.932
The finding writer can create
findings and nothing else.

39
00:02:30.932 --> 00:02:33.833
The verifier can close them,
which is why Chapter 5's

40
00:02:33.833 --> 00:02:36.871
business rule accepts a
close only from that account.

41
00:02:36.871 --> 00:02:39.786
And the evidence reader,
new in this workbook,

42
00:02:39.786 --> 00:02:43.333
can read a record but never
change one it's about to hash.

43
00:02:43.333 --> 00:02:44.883
Why custom roles at all?

44
00:02:44.883 --> 00:02:48.957
ServiceNow says the caller needs
roles that allow access to the table,

45
00:02:48.957 --> 00:02:52.620
and no single role allows
adding attachments by default.

46
00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:57.920
Two tools read that file.

47
00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:01.503
The first, kit calls, prints what
a task needs on each system,

48
00:03:01.503 --> 00:03:06.130
and with the granted option it compares
what an integration user actually

49
00:03:06.130 --> 00:03:10.832
holds against that need, exiting with
a failure on anything missing or extra.

50
00:03:10.832 --> 00:03:15.307
Grants are typed or exported by the
operator; it never touches the network.

51
00:03:15.307 --> 00:03:18.787
The second is the test that
holds the code to the list:

52
00:03:18.787 --> 00:03:23.642
it runs every client against a fake
HTTP session and checks each request,

53
00:03:23.642 --> 00:03:27.650
by method and whole path,
against the rows for that task.

54
00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:34.009
The chapter's own test
file has nine tests:

55
00:03:34.009 --> 00:03:37.923
every client's requests matched
to a declared row for its task,

56
00:03:37.923 --> 00:03:42.450
and the assertion that no task asks
SailPoint or Graph for a write scope.

57
00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:46.825
The kit's original ServiceNow
client tests still pass unchanged.

58
00:03:46.825 --> 00:03:49.523
Lab 1 runs seven of them: the base URLs,

59
00:03:49.523 --> 00:03:54.918
the order of OAuth before basic
authentication, Retry-After read both as

60
00:03:54.918 --> 00:04:00.670
seconds and as a date, a throttled
request retried, and every paging style.

61
00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:08.532
The vendor facts behind the code,
each read on November first.

62
00:04:08.532 --> 00:04:13.001
The token request is a post to the
instance's OAuth token endpoint

63
00:04:13.001 --> 00:04:17.057
with the grant type, client ID
and secret; a scope is optional.

64
00:04:17.057 --> 00:04:21.202
The Table API's read takes an
encoded query, a field list,

65
00:04:21.202 --> 00:04:25.070
a limit that defaults to
ten thousand, and an offset.

66
00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:28.947
The Attachment API needs the
table, the record and a file name,

67
00:04:28.947 --> 00:04:34.220
and a content type header, and no single
role allows adding attachments by default.

68
00:04:34.220 --> 00:04:38.290
And ServiceNow's Government
Community Cloud is FedRAMP High.

69
00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:43.465
Three points stay open.

70
00:04:43.465 --> 00:04:48.165
First, which inbound client-credentials
setup applies depends on the release:

71
00:04:48.165 --> 00:04:50.913
Zurich documents a
Machine Identity Console,

72
00:04:50.913 --> 00:04:54.890
and an older page documents a system
property that's off by default.

73
00:04:54.890 --> 00:04:59.661
Second, the token's optional scope:
ServiceNow's examples narrow incident

74
00:04:59.661 --> 00:05:04.353
reads and writes, but nothing found
says a scope can narrow a custom table.

75
00:05:04.353 --> 00:05:08.714
And third, API access policies:
whether an instance's policies

76
00:05:08.714 --> 00:05:12.990
add roles the kit doesn't list is
still on the kit's verify list.

77
00:05:12.990 --> 00:05:15.270
Lab 2 is where you settle them.

78
00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:20.133
What does this prove?

79
00:05:20.133 --> 00:05:25.843
That each integration user holds exactly
the roles its calls need and the code calls

80
00:05:25.843 --> 00:05:30.085
nothing else, which is least
privilege and account management,

81
00:05:30.085 --> 00:05:32.695
AC-6 and AC-2, in the Moderate baseline.

82
00:05:32.695 --> 00:05:35.770
Keep the comparison's output
with the access approval.

83
00:05:35.770 --> 00:05:39.797
That the account that closes
findings is separate from the ones

84
00:05:39.797 --> 00:05:43.445
that raise and read them, which
is separation of duties, AC-5.

85
00:05:43.445 --> 00:05:47.355
And that any change to what the
integration may call is reviewed,

86
00:05:47.355 --> 00:05:50.590
because it arrives as a
new row in a pull request.

87
00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:55.265
Who does what?

88
00:05:55.265 --> 00:05:59.778
Developers build or change the ServiceNow
side, and need to read Python.

89
00:05:59.778 --> 00:06:03.520
ServiceNow administrators
create the integration users,

90
00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:06.040
their OAuth clients and the three roles.

91
00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:08.304
And the I S S O approves the access,

92
00:06:08.304 --> 00:06:12.077
with the comparison's output
kept beside the approval.

93
00:06:12.077 --> 00:06:15.838
The rule for the users is
simple: three separate users,

94
00:06:15.838 --> 00:06:19.940
each with its own OAuth client
and exactly one custom role.

95
00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:23.271
None of them holds the broad
I T I L or admin roles,

96
00:06:23.271 --> 00:06:27.400
and the evidence reader
never borrows the verifier's.

97
00:06:31.150 --> 00:06:33.050
Lab 1 runs on a laptop.

98
00:06:33.050 --> 00:06:37.375
Step one proves every call is
declared: the nine tests pass.

99
00:06:37.375 --> 00:06:41.035
Step two lists what the re-read
client needs on ServiceNow:

100
00:06:41.035 --> 00:06:45.077
a token from its own OAuth client,
then a read of the finding table,

101
00:06:45.077 --> 00:06:47.975
a patch of one record,
and an attachment upload.

102
00:06:47.975 --> 00:06:51.750
Every one of those maps to
a single role, the verifier.

103
00:06:51.750 --> 00:06:54.580
That's the whole of what
this account should hold.

104
00:06:58.330 --> 00:06:59.855
Now catch the mistakes.

105
00:06:59.855 --> 00:07:04.305
An administrator gave the verifier
the I T I L role as well.

106
00:07:04.305 --> 00:07:07.898
The comparison finds nothing
missing, one role extra,

107
00:07:07.898 --> 00:07:11.818
says least privilege doesn't
hold, and exits with a failure.

108
00:07:11.818 --> 00:07:15.654
Then try the evidence export
reusing the verifier's account.

109
00:07:15.654 --> 00:07:19.880
It would work, which is the trap: it
would also be able to close findings.

110
00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:24.555
The tool reports the evidence reader role
missing and the verifier role extra.

111
00:07:24.555 --> 00:07:29.160
Finally, the original ServiceNow
client tests: seven pass.

112
00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:36.640
Lab 2 builds the split for real
on your developer instance;

113
00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:38.272
the authors haven't run it.

114
00:07:38.272 --> 00:07:42.222
Create the three custom roles with
exactly the grants on the slide.

115
00:07:42.222 --> 00:07:47.198
Create one integration user per
role, each with its own OAuth client.

116
00:07:47.198 --> 00:07:51.035
Compare each user's roles with
the tool, one task at a time.

117
00:07:51.035 --> 00:07:55.212
Then exercise them: get a token as
the verifier and list findings,

118
00:07:55.212 --> 00:07:58.285
and try to patch a record
as the evidence reader.

119
00:07:58.285 --> 00:07:59.735
That should be refused.

120
00:07:59.735 --> 00:08:05.900
Finally, delete the integrations, users
and roles, and revoke any saved token.

121
00:08:09.650 --> 00:08:13.175
The dangers are mostly about
privilege creeping in sideways.

122
00:08:13.175 --> 00:08:17.431
A client gains a call nobody listed,
and someone grants a broad role in

123
00:08:17.431 --> 00:08:21.837
production to make the error go away;
the test stops that before release.

124
00:08:21.837 --> 00:08:27.013
One user for everything lets the evidence
reader close findings; the split stops it.

125
00:08:27.013 --> 00:08:30.701
A broad role granted to make
it work is caught as an extra.

126
00:08:30.701 --> 00:08:36.000
Basic authentication outside a developer
instance puts a password in a pipeline.

127
00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:38.888
A secret in the config file is refused.

128
00:08:38.888 --> 00:08:42.850
And paging is tested, so no
waiting finding is skipped.

129
00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:47.950
Here's what done looks like.

130
00:08:47.950 --> 00:08:51.300
Every ServiceNow call has
a row and the test passes.

131
00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:55.388
Each integration user's roles
match the tool's list exactly.

132
00:08:55.388 --> 00:08:58.310
The finding writer, the
verifier and the evidence

133
00:08:58.310 --> 00:09:01.588
reader are separate users
with separate OAuth clients.

134
00:09:01.588 --> 00:09:03.000
None holds a broad role.

135
00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:06.838
Basic authentication exists
only on a developer instance,

136
00:09:06.838 --> 00:09:11.537
and tokens come from a vault or the
client-credentials grant, never a file.

137
00:09:11.537 --> 00:09:15.108
Next, Chapter 3 adds the
SailPoint and Graph rows

138
00:09:15.108 --> 00:09:18.588
and compares an API client's
scopes the same way.

139
00:09:18.588 --> 00:09:19.740
Thanks for watching.
